this is an interesting story that I  thought I'd bring up with you today  there's been a debate recently over the  claim that Elizabeth Warren predicted  the 2008 financial crisis before it  happened and most of the focus on the  story has been on the wrong thing most  of the focus has been on determining did  Elizabeth Warren predict the financial  crisis or did she not actually predict  the financial crisis but I don't  actually think that that's the right  argument at all to be having I'll tell  you the facts first and then I'll tell  you why I think the entire conversation  is being framed incorrectly 2020  Democratic candidate Elizabeth Warren  did a CNN Town Hall and one of the  things that she said was that she  predicted the 2008 financial crisis she  said quote and starting in the early  2000s the crisis was coming I was waving  my arms ringing the bell doing  everything I could I said families are  getting cheated all over this country it  started when the mortgage companies  targeted communities of color they  targeted seniors they targeted Latinos  they came in and sold the worst-possible  mortgages and stripped wealth out of  those communities and then took those  products across the nation I went  everywhere I could I talked about it to  anyone who would listen a crisis is  coming so is this true that she did this  basically it is okay before 2008  Elizabeth Warren was talking about the  impending housing and financial crisis  in her 2003 book she talked about it  subprime lending and housing crisis in  2004 on PBS she said that the growing  household debt the growing number of  risky mortgage lending practices are  causing a problem in 2005 she told the  New York Times that the amount of home  equity debt that homeowners were getting  into was a big red flag and PolitiFact  looked at this and determined yes  Elizabeth Warren did predict the  financial crisis but here's my reaction  so what Elizabeth Warren did predict the  financial crisis but why is that a big  deal tons of people predicted it tons  are predicting another crisis now and  none of it is even remotely notable it  requires special insight to predict but  what I mean by that is obviously our  economics  and circumstances are not sustainable of  course there's always a financial crisis  or a major downturn somewhere on the  horizon the question is is it a year  away or four years away or eight years  away I don't know how notable it is that  Elizabeth Warren predicted something  that anyone who understands the  boom-and-bust cycles of our economic  system has been predicting as a matter  of fact for decades or for as long as  they've been following the economy these  boom-bust cycles are implicit and  inherent to the economic system that we  have they're always on the horizon the  question is just when so one of the  reactions to this entire Elizabeth  Warren thing has been well we would care  about whether she did or didn't predict  it because we should want a president  who has the foresight to predict when  the next crisis will be well okay that  may be but anybody who understands how  the economy works should understand that  there's always a looming crisis on the  horizon there's always a looming  downturn on the horizon because it's  implicit to the system we have the  vulture capitalist cycles of boom and  bust that increase inequality an  increased despair don't happen less  frequently unless we actually deal with  the problems inherent to the system that  we have so Elizabeth Warren predicted it  tons of people predicted it and anybody  who understands our system understands  that such a crisis is always on the  horizon it's great that Elizabeth Warren  is actually talking about it but having  the foresight to predict it is actually  not notable although I'm glad that it is  getting more attention
 

